# 🌐 Router-on-a-Stick (ROAS) — Inter-VLAN Routing Lab

## 📌 Lab Overview

This lab demonstrates how to configure **Router-on-a-Stick (ROAS)** to enable communication between devices in different VLANs using a single physical router interface.

By default, VLANs create separate Layer 2 broadcast domains. Devices in different VLANs require a Layer 3 device to communicate.

In this lab, I configured VLANs, access ports, 802.1Q trunk links, and router subinterfaces to establish inter-VLAN communication.

**Lab status:** ✅ Completed — connectivity verified using ping tests and Cisco IOS command outputs.

## 🎯 Lab Objectives

- Create and configure VLANs on Cisco switches.
- Assign switch access ports to the appropriate VLANs.
- Configure 802.1Q trunk links.
- Configure router subinterfaces for inter-VLAN routing.
- Assign IPv4 addresses and default gateways.
- Verify VLAN and trunk configurations.
- Test connectivity within and between VLANs.
- Practice basic network troubleshooting.

